<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Safi of Persia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1629">1629</a>–<a href="/years/1642">1642</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Shah Safi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Safi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, known as Shah Safi, was the Safavid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1629 to 1642 and the grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>. His reign saw purges of officials and members of the royal family and the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> place his reign at the start of the empire's gradual decline after the achievements of Abbas I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia.jpg" alt="Safi of Persia" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shah_Safi_I_of_Persia_(reigned_1629-1642)_on_horseback_carrying_a_mace(_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Safi was born Sam Mirza, the son of Mohammad Baqer Mirza, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>, and Dilaram Khanum, a Georgian wife. In 1615 Abbas had Mohammad Baqer killed, fearing a plot against his life. Over the next few years the suspicious Abbas killed or blinded his other sons, which left his grandson Safi as heir to the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Safi was crowned on 28 January 1629 at the age of eighteen. He eliminated anyone he regarded as a threat, executing almost all the Safavid royal princes as well as leading courtiers and generals. On the night of 20 February 1632, known as the Bloody Ma'bas, he had forty women of the imperial harem put to death. He paid little attention to government, had never learned to read or write properly and preferred wine and opium. He excluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> from the bureaucracy and let a coalition of concubines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulams</a> hold power in the last decade of his reign.</p><p>The leading political figure was Saru Taqi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a>, who became grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> in 1634 after Safi had his predecessor Mirza Taleb Khan Ordubadi killed. He raised revenue, passed the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> to the crown domain and placed his brother as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s enemies exploited the shah's perceived weakness. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> under Sultan Murad IV began incursions within a year of his accession, briefly occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> in 1634 and recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in 1638. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zuhab">Treaty of Zuhab</a> of 1639 ended the wars between the two empires. In 1638 Iran also lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> after its governor, Ali Mardan Khan, was dismissed. In 1636 Safi received a trade delegation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-duke-of-holstein-gottorp">Frederick III, Duke of Holstein-Gottorp</a>, which included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-olearius">Adam Olearius</a>, who published a book about the visit in 1647.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Safi died on 12 May 1642, related to heavy drinking, and was buried in Qom. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-ii-of-persia">Abbas II</a> succeeded him. According to one account, in Archangelo Lamberti's Relation de la Colchide ou Mengrellie (1654), he died in a drinking contest with a Georgian named Shedan Chiladze. He left a country smaller than the one he inherited. Later writers saw in his reign problems that afflicted the Safavid empire in decline, among them his failure to prepare the crown prince for rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Safi of Persia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His grandson <b>Shah Safi</b> (r. 1629–1642) began his reign by purging experienced generals, officials and royal relatives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Shah Safi</b>, the people put under royal commissioners in place of governors suffered from their rapacity and corruption.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Kashan, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1635" aria-label="Isfahan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5876 977 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Safi of Persia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6326 1247h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6326 1247h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1635">What was here in 1635? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
